#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106588} :activity/id #uuid "2af93617-2850-5d80-8e89-a45566d1d99b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049223 :topic/id #uuid "e4ba6b67-ce78-5f37-b120-6055b0e97c6c" :topic/math-academy-id 1319} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107137 :tutorial/content "The sum of the interior angles of *any* triangle equals $180^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/fc/fcc21c5e13ab095da35eeca128e3a82a075d649e205aa0e1683b6ab0e4a4cc4c.png)\n\nAs it turns out, there is a general rule for the sum of the interior angles that applies to all polygons:\n\n*The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).$*\n\nFor example, every triangle has ${\\color{blue}3}$ sides. Let's substitute $n={\\color{blue}3}$ into the formula to check that it works:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (3-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a triangle equals $180^\\circ,$ which we know is correct.\n\nLet's see some more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "8edf1574-04d8-5aea-839f-9c03c16e6ab5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6654 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6daf0d6e-d3ca-5943-93ac-6a8d0ae04d45" :step/math-academy-id 136665 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107138 :tutorial/content "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals \n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).\n$$\n\nEvery quadrilateral has $n={\\color{blue}4}$ sides. Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral equals $360^\\circ.$ \n\n![](images/a0/a0d6cd7aa4b4e7aba6451b32087fa6cf86e0f63d2ff70e68328a5ac9d4eddcfc.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1de6f70b-9797-56cc-b325-b5375efaf06b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6688 :tutorial/title "The Sum of the Interior Angles of a Quadrilateral"} :step/id #uuid "55ff9455-d9c5-5dfd-b9aa-7d7ad79f0ad7" :step/math-academy-id 136666 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106590}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106590 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105717 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105440 :question/id #uuid "757aacb7-c988-5f1f-9ae6-c69ebf1399ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-10772"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d6d5827-91a8-5fd4-85ea-7564e8f5396e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050127}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500366}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Interior Angles of a Quadrilateral"} :step/id #uuid "93903594-40c4-5f1f-b805-5569c124dd4b" :step/math-academy-id 136667 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106591}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107139 :tutorial/content "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals \n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).\n$$\n\nEvery pentagon has $n={\\color{blue}5}$ sides. Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & ={540}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon equals $540^\\circ.$ \n\n![](images/8d/8d0478edf5e40b947b06b7b6b0b726999b11ce39dc8af5daaf414f2bd2292647.png)\n\nWe can use the same formula to deduce the sum of the interior angles of hexagons, heptagons, octagons, and so forth." :tutorial/id #uuid "573fd3ba-e480-5a75-ba71-1fd15e977637" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6738 :tutorial/title "The Sum of the Interior Angles of a Pentagon"} :step/id #uuid "9d73fd08-d0b3-549f-8356-26bd528a081c" :step/math-academy-id 136670 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106592}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105718 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105438 :question/id #uuid "5f89d47f-4068-508e-9fdd-1f3768d53a92" :question/math-academy-id "e-10637"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bad93a16-17d4-50ff-89c8-f3d966df5f1a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050452}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511562}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unknown Quantities in Quadrilaterals and Pentagons"} :step/id #uuid "66ba6652-6516-5c96-9fc2-f1a279bbcdce" :step/math-academy-id 136671 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106593}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105719 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105439 :question/id #uuid "7d0dd769-07d8-58c6-a2c8-d6165ca5bb5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10638"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b111120-41dd-5a58-bcc5-b0b6fc78b384" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048354}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500348}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Sides of a Polygon Given the Sum of Its Interior Angles"} :step/id #uuid "7f2b2f6c-0f00-518c-8edf-15031fffc70c" :step/math-academy-id 136674 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107170 :tutorial/content "We've made use of the following theorem throughout this lesson. \n\n*The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).$*\n\nThis formula works because any $n$-sided polygon can be split into $n-2$ triangles using diagonals that meet at one fixed vertex of the polygon. \n\nLet's illustrate this for a few different polygons.\n\n| ![](images/20/20aae267ba75bb418e509d548b1519af47b4cf9464d7f8400369c8e77b013aae.png) | **Quadrilateral** \n\nNumber of vertices: $4$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $4 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{2}} = 360^\\circ$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![](images/20/20551ad9f4661402aef9e501bf9d4c841e671b4277fae78f85d9e9ada9830592.png) | **Pentagon** \n\nNumber of vertices: $5$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $5 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{3}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}} = 540^\\circ$ |\n| ![](images/de/de8ec9be742a40384891de3a682a151e817f6c24d624e0ef505fb98ce6a1e60d.png) | **Hexagon** \n\nNumber of vertices: $6$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $6 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{4}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{4}} = 720^\\circ$ |\n\nThe sum of the interior angles of each polygon is the same as the sum of the interior angles of the corresponding triangles. \n\nEach triangle has interior angles that sum to $180^\\circ,$ and any $n$-sided polygon can be split into $n-2$ triangles in the manner shown above. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles in an $n$-sided polygon is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "211863f5-73c2-57af-83e9-696d21971d5b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9114 :tutorial/title "Justification of the Formula"} :step/id #uuid "19ab5e9d-cd9a-5abe-b2d8-a8c2a4b0511a" :step/math-academy-id 136677}] :activity/title "Interior Angles of Polygons" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105973}]}
